**CSS Grid — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
Grid divides a container into rows and columns and places children into the cells.
```html
<div class="gallery"><div>1</div><div>2</div><div>3</div></div>
```
```css
.gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 10px; }
```
Why it works: `repeat(3, 1fr)` creates three equal columns; `fr` units share the leftover space.

[Intermediate]
Use `auto-fit` + `minmax` for responsive card grids with no media queries.
```css
.cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }
```
Why it works: the browser fits as many 220px-minimum columns as possible and stretches them.

[Advanced]
Name your areas to build page layouts that read like diagrams.
```css
.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-areas: 'header header' 'sidebar main' 'footer footer';
  grid-template-columns: 240px 1fr;
  min-height: 100vh;
}
.page > header { grid-area: header; }
.page > aside { grid-area: sidebar; }
.page > main { grid-area: main; }
```
Why it works: each `grid-area` string maps children to named regions of the template.

[Expert]
Combine Grid with `subgrid`, `min-content` sizing and `align-content: space-between` for component-level and page-level layout in one system.
```css
.card { display: grid; grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto; gap: .75rem; }
```
Why it works: fixed header/footer rows with a `1fr` middle row pin actions to the bottom regardless of content height.

**async/await — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
An `async` function always returns a promise, and `await` pauses inside it until a promise settles.
```js
async function getUser() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/user');
  return res.json();
}
getUser().then(console.log);
```
Why it works: `await` unwraps the promise value, so the code reads top-to-bottom like sync code.

[Intermediate]
Always handle failures with `try/catch`, and check `res.ok` since fetch does not throw on 404/500.
```js
async function getUser() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('/api/user');
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed:', err.message);
    return null;
  }
}
```
Why it works: the catch block receives network errors and thrown HTTP errors in one place.

[Advanced]
Run independent awaits concurrently with `Promise.all`, or `allSettled` when partial failure is acceptable.
```js
const [user, posts] = await Promise.all([
  fetch('/api/user').then(r => r.json()),
  fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json())
]);
```
Why it works: both requests start immediately and the await finishes when the slowest settles.

[Expert]
Control lifetime with `AbortController` and timeouts, and serialize only when there is a real dependency.
```js
const ctrl = new AbortController();
const timer = setTimeout(() => ctrl.abort(), 5000);
try {
  const res = await fetch('/api/search?q=css', { signal: ctrl.signal });
  clearTimeout(timer);
  return await res.json();
} catch (e) {
  return { error: e.name === 'AbortError' ? 'timeout' : 'network' };
}
```
Why it works: aborting cancels the in-flight request, freeing the connection instead of waiting forever.

**DOM events — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
Listen with `addEventListener` and react inside the callback.
```html
<button id="save">Save</button>
```
```js
document.querySelector('#save').addEventListener('click', () => {
  console.log('saved!');
});
```
Why it works: the browser calls your function every time the event fires on that element.

[Intermediate]
Use event delegation: one listener on a parent handles clicks for all current and future children.
```js
document.querySelector('#list').addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const btn = e.target.closest('button.item');
  if (btn) console.log('clicked item', btn.dataset.id);
});
```
Why it works: clicks bubble up, and `closest()` finds the real target even on inner nodes.

[Advanced]
Handle inputs correctly: use `submit` with `preventDefault()` for forms, debounce expensive handlers, and clean up with `removeEventListener`.
```js
const form = document.querySelector('#search');
form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  doSearch(new FormData(form));
});
```
Why it works: preventing default stops the page reload, and `FormData` collects all named fields.

[Expert]
Know the event lifecycle — capture, target, bubble — and use `{ once: true }`, `CustomEvent` with `detail`, and passive listeners for scroll performance.
```js
window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });
el.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cart:add', { detail: { id: 7 } }));
```
Why it works: passive listeners promise not to call `preventDefault()`, letting the browser scroll without waiting for JS.

**fetch API — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
`fetch(url)` returns a promise resolving to a Response; parse the body with `.json()` or `.text()`.
```js
fetch('/api/todos')
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data));
```
Why it works: the body stream must be consumed explicitly, and `.json()` decodes it for you.

[Intermediate]
Send data with `method`, `headers` and a JSON body; treat non-2xx as errors yourself.
```js
await fetch('/api/todos', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ title: 'Learn fetch', done: false })
});
```
Why it works: `JSON.stringify` serializes the payload and the header tells the server how to parse it.

[Advanced]
Build a small wrapper that adds timeouts, error normalization and JSON handling once.
```js
async function api(path, options = {}) {
  const res = await fetch(path, {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    ...options
  });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(res.status + ' ' + res.statusText);
  return res.status === 204 ? null : res.json();
}
```
Why it works: every call shares the same error contract, so UI code never repeats boilerplate.

[Expert]
Use `AbortController` for cancellation, cache with the `Cache` API or HTTP caching headers, and stream large responses with `res.body.getReader()`.
```js
const cache = await caches.open('api-v1');
const cached = await cache.match('/api/todos');
const res = cached || await fetch('/api/todos');
if (!cached) cache.put('/api/todos', res.clone());
```
Why it works: `clone()` lets the same response be consumed twice — once for cache, once for the UI.

**CSS animations — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
`@keyframes` defines states; `animation` applies them over time.
```css
.fade-in { animation: fadeIn .6s ease both; }
@keyframes fadeIn { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }
```
Why it works: the browser interpolates `opacity` between the keyframes automatically.

[Intermediate]
Chain multiple properties and use `animation-delay` with `steps()` for sprite-style effects.
```css
.pulse {
  animation: pulse 1.2s ease-in-out infinite;
}
@keyframes pulse {
  0%, 100% { transform: scale(1); box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 rgb(0 120 255 / .4); }
  50% { transform: scale(1.05); box-shadow: 0 0 0 12px rgb(0 120 255 / 0); }
}
```
Why it works: percentage keyframes let one timeline animate transform and shadow together.

[Advanced]
Animate only `transform` and `opacity` (compositor-friendly), and gate motion with `prefers-reduced-motion`.
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  * { animation-duration: .01ms !important; animation-iteration-count: 1 !important; }
}
```
Why it works: those properties skip layout and paint, and the media query respects users with vestibular disorders.

[Expert]
Coordinate complex timelines with CSS variables and `animationend` events in JS.
```css
.slide { --dur: .4s; animation: slide var(--dur) cubic-bezier(.2,.8,.2,1) both; }
@keyframes slide { from { transform: translateX(100%); } to { transform: translateX(0); } }
```
```js
card.addEventListener('animationend', () => card.classList.add('is-shown'));
```
Why it works: custom properties parameterize duration, and `animationend` synchronizes JS state with the visual finish.

**Responsive design — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
Start with the viewport meta tag and fluid widths so layout adapts before any media query.
```html
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
```
```css
.container { width: min(100% - 2rem, 1100px); margin-inline: auto; }
```
Why it works: `min()` caps width on big screens while staying fluid on small ones.

[Intermediate]
Use mobile-first media queries with `min-width` and relative units (`rem`, `%`, `ch`).
```css
.grid { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
@media (min-width: 48rem) {
  .grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
}
```
Why it works: the base style targets phones; larger screens progressively add columns.

[Advanced]
Prefer intrinsic patterns — `auto-fit/minmax`, `clamp()`, flex-wrap — over breakpoint lists.
```css
.cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); }
h1 { font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 4vw, 3.5rem); }
```
Why it works: components reflow at any width, so container changes work without viewport queries.

[Expert]
Make components container-aware with container queries and test with real content in both languages.
```css
.card-wrap { container-type: inline-size; }
@container (min-width: 400px) {
  .card { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 96px 1fr; }
}
```
Why it works: the card reacts to the space it is actually given, not to the whole viewport.

**CSS transitions — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
A transition animates a property change between two states.
```css
.button { background: #2563eb; transition: background .2s ease; }
.button:hover { background: #1d4ed8; }
```
Why it works: when the hover state applies, the browser interpolates the background over 0.2s.

[Intermediate]
Transition specific properties with per-property durations and easings.
```css
.card { transition: transform .25s cubic-bezier(.2,.8,.2,1), box-shadow .25s ease; }
.card:hover { transform: translateY(-4px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgb(0 0 0 / .15); }
```
Why it works: listing properties separately avoids animating everything and keeps motion intentional.

[Advanced]
Animate only compositor-friendly properties (`transform`, `opacity`) and add `will-change` sparingly for known hot paths.
```css
.modal { opacity: 0; transform: scale(.96); transition: opacity .2s ease, transform .2s ease; }
.modal.is-open { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1); }
```
Why it works: toggling a class animates GPU-composited layers without triggering layout.

[Expert]
Respect reduced motion and trigger exit animations before removing nodes.
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .card, .modal { transition: none; }
}
```
```js
el.addEventListener('transitionend', () => el.remove(), { once: true });
```
Why it works: `transitionend` fires exactly when the visual exit completes, so removal never looks like a jump.

**Building a navbar with Flexbox — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
A navbar is a flex row: brand on one side, links and actions on the other.
```html
<nav class="nav"><a class="nav__brand" href="/">Site</a><ul class="nav__links"><li><a href="/docs">Docs</a></li></ul></nav>
```
```css
.nav { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; padding: .75rem 1rem; }
.nav__links { display: flex; gap: 1rem; list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
```
Why it works: `space-between` pushes the brand and link group to opposite edges.

[Intermediate]
Make it responsive by letting links wrap and collapsing into a menu at small widths.
```css
@media (max-width: 48rem) {
  .nav__links { flex-direction: column; width: 100%; display: none; }
  .nav.is-open .nav__links { display: flex; }
}
```
Why it works: toggling one class switches the same flex container between row and column layouts.

[Advanced]
Handle overflow safely: `min-width: 0` on items, ellipsis on long labels, focus styles for keyboard users.
```css
.nav a { min-width: 0; white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; }
.nav a:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--brand, #2563eb); outline-offset: 2px; }
```
Why it works: truncation prevents layout blowouts while `:focus-visible` keeps navigation usable without a mouse.

[Expert]
Ship it as an accessible landmark: skip-link target, `aria-expanded` on the toggle, and reduced-motion for the dropdown.
```html
<button class="nav__toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="menu">Menu</button>
```
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .nav__links { transition: none; } }
```
Why it works: assistive tech reads the toggle state from `aria-expanded`, so behavior and semantics stay in sync.

**Grid page layouts — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
Define the page as named rows: header, content, footer — and let main content flex.
```css
body { display: grid; grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto; min-height: 100vh; margin: 0; }
```
Why it works: the `1fr` middle row absorbs leftover height, pinning the footer to the bottom.

[Intermediate]
Add a sidebar with `grid-template-columns` and named areas for clarity.
```css
.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-areas: 'header header' 'side main' 'footer footer';
  grid-template-columns: minmax(200px, 260px) 1fr;
}
```
Why it works: areas describe the layout in one readable place; children claim slots by name.

[Advanced]
Collapse the sidebar on small screens by redefining the template instead of rewriting children.
```css
@media (max-width: 48rem) {
  .page { grid-template-areas: 'header' 'main' 'footer'; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .page > aside { display: none; }
}
```
Why it works: only the container changes; every child keeps its `grid-area` assignment.

[Expert]
Compose with `subgrid` so card internals align across the page grid.
```css
.cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
.card { display: grid; grid-template-rows: subgrid; grid-row: span 3; }
```
Why it works: rows inherit the parent track sizes, so titles and actions align perfectly across cards.

**Advanced fetch — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
Wrap fetch in a function so every call gets consistent error handling.
```js
async function getJSON(url) {
  const res = await fetch(url);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
  return res.json();
}
```
Why it works: centralizing the `res.ok` check turns silent 404s into catchable errors.

[Intermediate]
Add a timeout with `AbortSignal.timeout` or `AbortController`.
```js
async function getJSON(url, ms = 5000) {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(ms) });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
  return res.json();
}
```
Why it works: the signal aborts the request after `ms`, so hangs become timeout errors.

[Advanced]
Add exponential backoff retry for transient failures only (network, 502/503/429).
```js
async function retry(fn, tries = 3) {
  for (let i = 0; i < tries; i++) {
    try { return await fn(); }
    catch (e) {
      if (i === tries - 1) throw e;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 2 ** i * 300));
    }
  }
}
const data = await retry(() => getJSON('/api/list'));
```
Why it works: waiting 300ms, 600ms, 1200ms gives a struggling server room to recover.

[Expert]
Cancel stale requests when inputs change (search-as-you-type).
```js
let ctrl;
input.addEventListener('input', async () => {
  ctrl?.abort();
  ctrl = new AbortController();
  try {
    const res = await fetch('/api/search?q=' + encodeURIComponent(input.value), { signal: ctrl.signal });
    render(await res.json());
  } catch (e) { if (e.name !== 'AbortError') console.error(e); }
});
```
Why it works: each keystroke aborts the previous query, so results always match the newest input.

**CSS Grid برای layout صفحه — از مبتدی تا حرفه‌ای**

[مبتدی]
Grid صفحه را به سطر و ستون تقسیم می‌کند؛ با `fr` فضا را بین ستون‌ها تقسیم کن.
```css
.page { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 240px 1fr; gap: 1rem; }
```
چرا کار می‌کند: ستون `1fr` باقی فضا را می‌گیرد و ستون 240px ثابت می‌ماند.

[متوسط]
با `grid-template-areas` نقشه صفحه را مثل یک دیاگرام بنویس.
```css
.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-areas: 'header header' 'side main' 'footer footer';
  grid-template-columns: 240px 1fr;
}
.page > header { grid-area: header; }
.page > aside { grid-area: side; }
```
چرا کار می‌کند: هر فرزند با نام ناحیه‌ی خودش در ماتریس جای می‌گیرد.

[پیشرفته]
برای موبایل فقط template را بازتعریف کن؛ فرزندان دست‌نخورده می‌مانند.
```css
@media (max-width: 48rem) {
  .page { grid-template-areas: 'header' 'main' 'footer'; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}
```
چرا کار می‌کند: چیدمان در یک نقطه متمرکز است و تغییرش ریسک breaking ندارد.

[حرفه‌ای]
با `minmax()` و `auto` ستون‌ها را مقاوم کن و برای کارت‌ها از `subgrid` استفاده کن تا ارتفاع سطرها هم‌تراز شود.
```css
.page { grid-template-columns: minmax(200px, 24rem) minmax(0, 1fr); }
.card { display: grid; grid-template-rows: subgrid; grid-row: span 3; }
```
چرا کار می‌کند: `minmax(0, 1fr)` جلوی سرریز محتوای طولانی را می‌گیرد و subgrid تراز بین‌کارت را تضمین می‌کند.

**async/await — از مبتدی تا حرفه‌ای**

[مبتدی]
تابع `async` همیشه Promise برمی‌گرداند و `await` منتظر نتیجه می‌ماند.
```js
async function getUser() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/user');
  return res.json();
}
```
چرا کار می‌کند: `await` مقدار Promise را باز می‌کند تا کد خطی و خوانا بماند.

[متوسط]
خطاها را با `try/catch` بگیر و `res.ok` را چک کن چون fetch روی 404 خطا نمی‌دهد.
```js
try {
  const res = await fetch('/api/user');
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
  const user = await res.json();
} catch (err) {
  console.error('خطا:', err.message);
}
```
چرا کار می‌کند: هم خطای شبکه و هم خطای HTTP در یک catch جمع می‌شوند.

[پیشرفته]
درخواست‌های مستقل را با `Promise.all` همزمان اجرا کن تا منتظر یکی‌یکی شدن نمانی.
```js
const [user, posts] = await Promise.all([
  fetch('/api/user').then(r => r.json()),
  fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json())
]);
```
چرا کار می‌کند: هر دو درخواست بلافاصله ارسال می‌شوند و زمان کل برابر کندترین آن‌هاست.

[حرفه‌ای]
با `AbortController` درخواست‌های قدیمی را لغو کن؛ مخصوصاً در search-as-you-type.
```js
let ctrl;
input.addEventListener('input', async () => {
  ctrl?.abort();
  ctrl = new AbortController();
  const res = await fetch('/api/search?q=' + encodeURIComponent(input.value), { signal: ctrl.signal });
});
```
چرا کار می‌کند: هر ورودی جدید درخواست قبلی را cancel می‌کند تا نتیجه همیشه تازه باشد.

Hoist every repeated decision into tokens, then reference tokens everywhere — one edit re-themes the whole UI.

```css
:root {
  --brand: #2563eb;
  --danger: #dc2626;
  --space-1: .5rem;
  --space-2: 1rem;
  --radius: 8px;
}
.button { padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--brand); }
.alert { padding: var(--space-2); border-inline-start: 4px solid var(--danger); }
[data-theme='dark'] { --brand: #60a5fa; --danger: #f87171; }
```

- Repeated literals (`#2563eb`, `16px`) become named contracts.
- Variants (dark theme, density) are scope overrides, not new CSS.

Why it works: components consume semantic tokens, so the mapping from design decision to rendered value exists in exactly one place.

سه اهرم اصلی در سایت استاتیک: بارگذاری تنبل تصاویر، بارگذاری هوشمند فونت، و کاهش reflow.

```html
<img src="photo.webp" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="توضیح تصویر">
<link rel="preload" href="fonts/iransans.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
```

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: 'IRANSans';
  src: url('fonts/iransans.woff2') format('woff2');
  font-display: swap;
}
```

- `loading="lazy"` + width/height یعنی تصویر خارج از viewport دانلود نمی‌شود و CLS صفر می‌ماند.
- `font-display: swap` متن را با فونت جایگزین فوراً نشان می‌دهد تا صفحه منتظر فونت نماند.
- برای کاهش reflow، انیمیشن را به `transform` و `opacity` محدود کن.

چرا کار می‌کند: مرورگر فقط منابع لازم را دانلود می‌کند، متن زودتر دیده می‌شود و layout به‌جای هر فریم یک‌بار محاسبه می‌شود.

Define a small ramp of variables with semantic names, then forbid raw values in components — structure beats taste.

```css
:root {
  --gray-100: #f1f5f9; --gray-500: #64748b; --gray-900: #0f172a;
  --brand: #2563eb; --danger: #dc2626;
  --space-1: .5rem; --space-2: 1rem; --space-3: 2rem;
  --radius: 8px;
}
.surface { background: var(--gray-100); color: var(--gray-900); padding: var(--space-2); border-radius: var(--radius); }
.muted { color: var(--gray-500); }
```

- Palette: base neutrals + one brand + state colors (danger/success). Nothing else.
- Spacing: a 4px or 8px scale (`--space-1..3`), never arbitrary numbers.

Why it works: with named scales, spacing rhythm and contrast are decided once and audited in one file, while components stay swap-safe when the palette changes.

**What was wrong**
- Six inline styles duplicated on every button instance.
- Raw `blue` instead of a brand token.
- No hover/focus states possible from inline styles.

**Improved code**
```html
<button class="button">Submit</button>
```
```css
.button {
  background: var(--brand, #2563eb);
  color: #fff;
  padding: .625rem 1.25rem;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 8px;
  font-size: 1rem;
}
.button:hover { filter: brightness(.92); }
.button:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--brand, #2563eb); outline-offset: 2px; }
```

Why it's better: one reusable class, themeable through a custom property, with hover and keyboard-focus states that inline styles could never express.

**مشکل‌ها**
- `alert()` رابط کاربری را مسدود می‌کند و ظاهرش کنترل‌پذیر نیست.
- `var` به‌جای `const`/`let` و مقایسه با `==` به‌جای `===`.
- اعتبارسنجی و پیام باید در UI فرم باشند، نه در دیالوگ مرورگر.

**کد اصلاح‌شده**
```html
<form id="greet-form">
  <label for="name">نام</label>
  <input id="name" name="name" type="text" required>
  <p id="msg" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <button type="submit">سلام</button>
</form>
```
```js
const form = document.querySelector('#greet-form');
const msg = document.querySelector('#msg');
form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const name = form.name.value.trim();
  msg.textContent = name === '' ? 'خالی است' : 'سلام ' + name;
});
```

**چرا بهتر است:** پیام داخل صفحه و در دسترس screen reader است، فرم بومی اعتبارسنجی می‌کند، و کد از `const` و `===` استفاده می‌کند.

**What was wrong**
- Fixed `width: 960px` overflows any screen narrower than that.
- Pixel-only sizing ignores user font preferences.
- `float` layout is a fragile tool for the wrong job.

**Improved code**
```css
.wrap { width: min(100% - 2rem, 60rem); margin-inline: auto; padding: 1rem; font-size: 1.125rem; }
.layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.5rem; }
@media (min-width: 48rem) {
  .layout { grid-template-columns: 18rem 1fr; }
}
```

Why it's better: `min()` plus relative units make the page fluid by default, and a two-column grid appears only when there is room — no horizontal scrollbar at any width.

**What was wrong**
- A clickable `<div>` has no role, no keyboard focus, no Enter/Space handling.
- An `<a href="#">` fakes an action: it changes the URL hash and lies about navigation.
- Inline `onclick` handlers with global functions (`del`, `save`).

**Improved code**
```html
<button type="button" class="btn btn--danger" data-action="delete" data-id="3">Delete</button>
<button type="button" class="btn" data-action="save">Save</button>
```
```js
document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const btn = e.target.closest('button[data-action]');
  if (!btn) return;
  if (btn.dataset.action === 'delete') removeItem(btn.dataset.id);
  if (btn.dataset.action === 'save') saveForm();
});
```

Why it's better: real buttons are focusable and keyboard-operable for free, semantics match behavior, and one delegated listener replaces repeated inline handlers.

**مشکل‌ها**
- Callback nesting عمیق: خوانایی و مدیریت خطا از بین رفته.
- هیچ try/catch یا مسیر خطا برای هر مرحله وجود ندارد.
- ترتیب وابستگی‌ها ضمنی است.

**کد اصلاح‌شده**
```js
async function loadAndRender(id) {
  const user = await getUserAsync(id);
  const orders = await getOrdersAsync(user);
  const items = await getItemsAsync(orders);
  render(items);
}

// اگر APIهای قدیمی فقط callback می‌پذیرند:
const getUserAsync = (id) => new Promise((res, rej) => getUser(id, res, rej));
```

اگر مراحل مستقل‌اند، موازی اجرا کن:
```js
const [user, settings] = await Promise.all([getUserAsync(id), getSettingsAsync()]);
```

**چرا بهتر است:** کد خطی و تخت می‌شود، خطا با try/catch در یک نقطه جمع می‌شود، و Promise.wrapping کد قدیمی را بدون بازنویسی کتابخانه سازگار می‌کند.

**What was wrong**
- The same declaration block copied three times — a change now needs three edits.
- Magic values (`#ddd`, `4px`, `16px`) with no shared meaning.

**Improved code**
```css
:root {
  --surface-border: #e2e8f0;
  --radius: 8px;
  --space-2: 1rem;
  --surface: #fff;
}
.surface,
.card,
.panel,
.modal {
  border: 1px solid var(--surface-border);
  border-radius: var(--radius);
  padding: var(--space-2);
  background: var(--surface);
}
```
```html
<div class="card surface">...</div>
```

Why it's better: the shared recipe lives once (single selector list or a composable `surface` class), and every value is a token — restyling all containers is a one-line change.

**What was wrong**
- Four levels of nesting; control flow reads inside-out.
- `fail` repeated three times with no context about which step failed.
- No way to compose or cancel steps.

**Improved code**
```js
const promisify = (fn) => (...args) =>
  new Promise((resolve, reject) => fn(...args, resolve, reject));

const loadConfigP = promisify(loadConfig);
const connectP = promisify(connect);
const subscribeP = promisify(subscribe);

async function start() {
  try {
    const config = await loadConfigP();
    const session = await connectP(config);
    const channel = await subscribeP(session);
    channel.on('message', handle);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('startup failed:', err);
    showFatalError(err);
  }
}
```

Why it's better: the flow is a flat, readable sequence, every step's failure lands in one `catch` with stack context, and each promisified step is independently testable and reusable.

**What was wrong**
- Fixed pixel sizes for type, spacing and columns — nothing adapts below ~1100px.
- Three hard-coded column widths overflow on any small screen.
- A stack of breakpoints would be needed to patch every size by hand.

**Improved code**
```css
h2 { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1rem + 2.5vw, 2.25rem); }
.hero {
  max-width: 75rem;
  padding: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 5rem) clamp(1rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
  margin-inline: auto;
}
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 20rem), 1fr));
  gap: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
}
```

Why it's better: `clamp()` and `auto-fit`/`minmax` let type, spacing and column count respond intrinsically to available space — one rule set that works from 320px to 4K with zero breakpoints.

**مشکل‌ها**
- دکمه با `div`: بدون role، بدون فوکوس کیبورد، بدون Space/Enter.
- لینک با `span`: ناوبری واقعی ندارد و در middle-click یا bookmark کار نمی‌کند.
- منطق با onclick inline و توابع global.

**کد اصلاح‌شده**
```html
<button type="button" class="btn" data-action="like">پسندیدم</button>
<a class="link" href="/docs">مستندات</a>
```
```js
document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const btn = e.target.closest('[data-action="like"]');
  if (btn) toggleLike();
});
```

**چرا بهتر است:** `button` فوکوس و فعال‌سازی کیبورد بومی دارد، `a href` ناوبری واقعی (باز کردن در تب جدید، bookmark) می‌دهد، و delegation یک listener برای همه دکمه‌ها کافی است.

**What was wrong**
- Three selectors share an identical declaration block — copy-paste CSS drifts the first time someone edits one copy.
- Magic values (`#1e293b`, `22px`, `12px`) have no shared meaning or scale.

**Improved code**
```css
:root {
  --text-strong: #1e293b;
  --fs-heading: 1.375rem;
  --space-2: 1rem;
}
.heading,
.t1,
.t2,
.price {
  color: var(--text-strong);
  font-size: var(--fs-heading);
  margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
}
.price { font-weight: 700; }
```
```html
<h2 class="heading">Plan title</h2>
<p class="heading price">$9</p>
```

Why it's better: one declaration block (selector list) plus one composable modifier means a single source of truth, and tokens make the next theme change a two-line edit instead of a grep-and-pray hunt.
